      "text": "First, let's write the standard form of the hyperbola equation and identify the values of $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe given equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\n\\]\n\nThis equation represents a hyperbola that opens vertically, meaning its standard form is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nBy comparing the given equation with the standard form, we can determine $a^2$ and $b^2$:\n\n\\[\na^2 = 27 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = 11\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\na = \\sqrt{27} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = \\sqrt{11}\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the vertices of a hyperbola is given by $2a$.\n\nTherefore, the distance between the vertices is:\n\n\\[\n2a = 2\\sqrt{27}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's calculate $2\\sqrt{27}$.\n\n\\[\n2\\sqrt{27} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{27} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 3} = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nTo confirm the accuracy of our result, we can use Python to compute it.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Given values of a^2 and b^2\na_squared = 27\nb_squared = 11\n\n# Calculate a\na = math.sqrt(a_squared)\n\n# Distance between the vertices\ndistance_between_vertices = 2 * a\nprint(distance_between_vertices)\n```",
